+\subsection{Custody Accounting}
+
+Let $L$ be cumulative locked native value, $D$ cumulative redemption value already credited after verified burns, and $P$ cumulative value actually paid to claimants, including relayer shares. Define outstanding backing $O=L-D$ and unpaid credits $C=D-P$. Then
+\[
+ \mathsf{vaultBalance}=O+C+X=L-P+X,\qquad 0\le P\le D\le L,
+\]
+where $X$ accounts for unexpected incoming value separately. Neither credits nor $X$ create a new mint entitlement. The Execution-layer circulating supply is at most $O$, allowing locks not yet minted and burns not yet redeemed. It is not generally equal to vault balance and is not automatically observable by one global query in a peer-to-peer execution system.
+
+The invariants are checked over local ledgers and complete controlled histories in tests. Public monitoring reports known liabilities and coverage of off-chain observations rather than claiming exhaustive circulating-supply measurement. One-way launch is not a public bridge milestone; both mint backing and redemption must work.
 
