 \chapter{Introduction}
 
-By \emph{governance}\index{governance}, we mean the operational management and re-configuration of a running Unicity Platform instance: the validator sets of the BFT Core and of the partitions, the lifecycle of partitions and shards, incentives to participants, and updates to the system. Governance parameters and algorithms change only through governance.
-
-Governance is architected in a modular way. The \emph{Governance Body}\index{Governance Body} is an abstract entity that produces change records consumed by the rest of the platform. It may implement different mechanisms, Proof of Authority or Proof of Stake, behind the same record formats. The Governance Body implements a number of independent \emph{Governance Processes}\index{Governance Process}, each defined by the records it produces.
-
-On the Unicity mainnet the Governance Body is instantiated as a set of contracts hosted by the enshrined EVM partition (Chapter~\ref{ch:evm}), called the \emph{governance partition}\index{governance partition} in this chapter. Its state is certified by the BFT Core every round, and the BFT Core's own seals are fed back into it, so the two layers observe each other through certified data alone.
+Governance manages validator assignments, partition configurations, incentives and protocol upgrades. The \emph{Governance Body}\index{Governance Body} produces authenticated change records. Under PoA these originate from a disclosed authority; under PoS they are outputs of contracts executing on the enshrined governance EVM shard (Chapter~\ref{ch:evm}). Root consensus adopts valid changes; possession of an agent key or access to a configuration API is not a separate authority to choose the set.
 
+The initial public currency deployment may remain under PoA. Authoritative PoS is activated only after weighted consensus, the epoch handoff, objective slashing, retirement protection, forced inclusion and checkpoint recovery are enabled and validated together. Merely running staking contracts or observing their output in shadow mode does not activate PoS.
 
 \chapter{Data Flow}
 
-This chapter describes the ``control plane'' of the Unicity Distributed Machine, complementing the ``data plane'' of user transaction processing.
-
 \section{Governance Body}
 
-The Governance Body manages the configuration of a Unicity Network. It produces \emph{change records}\index{change record}, whose formats per Governance Process are documented in Chapter~\ref{ch:gov-processes}. A change record becomes effective when the component it addresses adopts it at an epoch boundary (Sec.~\ref{sec:bft-epoch-change}).
-
-\subsection{How a Validator joins a Partition}
-
-Validators rely on \emph{configuration agents}\index{configuration agent} for performing steps that involve user-level communication with other partitions and shards. The process works like this:
-
-\begin{itemize}
-	\item The configuration agent detects a new change record it is involved with, and learns the partition identifier and shard identifier.
-	\item The configuration agent obtains the current epoch number from the shard.
-	\item The configuration agent obtains the change records from the current one to the joining one, if not yet known.
-	\item The configuration agent activates the node process, providing the necessary configuration.
-	\item The node continues as described in Sec.~\ref{sec:shard-node-joining}.
-\end{itemize}
+Each BFT Core operator runs a paired execution node. A configuration agent may obtain certified records, proofs and state snapshots from any available source. It verifies these inputs before proposing a change or starting a joining node (Sec.~\ref{sec:shard-node-joining}). Proof verification itself does not require an execution client; producing and validating EVM blocks does.
 
 \section{Information Flows}\label{sec:gov-flows}
 
-Four flows connect the governance partition to the rest of the platform. Each is authenticated by certified data, and none relies on a trusted intermediary.
-
 \begin{description}
-	\item[Seal feed] Consensus Layer to governance partition. Every block of the governance partition records the Unicity Seal of the partition's previous round, its signer set, and the trust base of the seal's epoch (Sec.~\ref{sec:evm-seal-feed}). This is the source of the certified round clock, of participation accounting (Sec.~\ref{sec:gov-rewards}), and of the trust base used by built-in verification.
-	\item[Validator assignment] Governance partition to Consensus Layer. The elected validator set of the next epoch is committed in the certified state of the governance partition. Each BFT Core validator reads and verifies it against a state root it has itself certified, and co-signs the resulting Unicity Trust Base record (Sec.~\ref{sec:gov-trust-base}). No validator requires an execution node for this step.
-	\item[Evidence] Consensus Layer to governance partition. A validator that observes conflicting consensus votes submits them as a transaction; verification is by built-in function (Sec.~\ref{sec:gov-slashing}). Submission is permissionless.
-	\item[Value] Between the governance partition and the Execution Layer, in both directions, by lock on the partition and mint on the Execution Layer, and by burn on the Execution Layer and release on the partition (Sec.~\ref{sec:bridge-enshrined}).
+ \item[Root origin and acknowledgement.] The root sends certified progress, configuration transitions and the governance-shard assignment through the mandatory state feed. EVM clocks observe root progress at EVM execution boundaries, not at every root tick.
+ \item[Candidate and adoption.] Contracts commit an election candidate in EVM storage. An agent supplies its body and state proof to the root, which validates the certified output and commits the handoff. The root's adoption acknowledgement returns through the feed.
+ \item[Evidence.] Anyone may submit authenticated conflicting consensus votes to the slashing contract. A bounded forced-inclusion path protects access against an EVM producer.
